# PART 4 — HYBRID MODULAR ARCHITECTURE

This is the central architectural requirement.

Use a shared design foundation with independently importable modules and optional advanced capabilities.

## 4.1 Architecture layers

### Layer 1: Design Foundation

Contains:

- Design tokens.
- Typography.
- Colors.
- Semantic colors.
- Spacing.
- Sizing.
- Borders.
- Radius.
- Shadows.
- Elevation.
- Motion.
- Breakpoints.
- Z-index.
- Focus styles.
- Icon conventions.
- Theme infrastructure.
- Responsive utilities.
- Accessibility primitives.

### Layer 2: Core Components

Contains commonly used reusable components:

- Buttons.
- Inputs.
- Labels.
- Selects.
- Checkboxes.
- Radio groups.
- Sliders.
- Switches.
- Cards.
- Badges.
- Tooltips.
- Popovers.
- Dialogs.
- Drawers.
- Menus.
- Tabs.
- Tables.
- Alerts.
- Progress indicators.
- Loading indicators.

### Layer 3: Standard Modules

Independently importable groups:

- Navigation.
- Workspace.
- Forms.
- Data display.
- Analytics.
- Communication.
- Collaboration.
- Scheduling.
- Business components.
- Administration.
- File management.
- System feedback.

### Layer 4: Advanced Modules

Optional complex experiences:

- Workflow builder.
- Form builder.
- Page builder.
- Dashboard builder.
- Report builder.
- Rich text editor.
- Advanced charting.
- AI interface system.
- Visual automation editor.
- Data exploration tools.
- Media editing.
- Diagramming.

### Layer 5: Adapters and Extensions

Provide adapters for optional external libraries and platform capabilities.

Examples:

- Charting libraries.
- Maps.
- Rich text editors.
- Code editors.
- PDF viewers.
- Diagram engines.
- Media processing.
- 3D rendering.
- Virtualization.

Do not couple the core package to one provider or library when an adapter boundary is appropriate.

## 4.2 Hybrid import model

Design an import model that supports:

- Main package imports.
- Feature-level imports.
- Independently importable advanced modules.
- Optional external integrations.
- Tree-shaking.
- Lazy loading where appropriate.
- TypeScript declarations.
- CSS and theme imports.

Do not force consumers to install or bundle every advanced capability.

Do not create excessive package fragmentation.

Use the existing repository's package structure and build system as the starting point.

If package restructuring or multiple publishable packages are proposed, document the design, compatibility impact, licensing implications and migration plan before implementing breaking changes.

## 4.3 Future module contract

Create a documented standard for adding new modules.

Every future module must define:

- Purpose.
- Public API.
- Component exports.
- TypeScript interfaces.
- Dependencies.
- Design tokens.
- Theme behavior.
- Responsive behavior.
- Accessibility behavior.
- Interaction states.
- Loading states.
- Empty states.
- Error states.
- Storybook documentation.
- Unit tests.
- Integration tests.
- Visual regression coverage.
- Bundle-size impact.
- Compatibility expectations.

A new module must automatically inherit the Wendoro design language.

